WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:03.971
In this first session, we'll explore

2
00:00:04.051 --> 00:00:07.441
what statistical analysis involves. Who
exactly are we studying?

3
00:00:07.521 --> 00:00:11.184
What is the population of interest? And,
crucially,

4
00:00:11.264 --> 00:00:14.791
what data can we access, and what can they
tell us about the wider population?

5
00:00:14.871 --> 00:00:16.845
We always start with a research question.

6
00:00:16.925 --> 00:00:19.402
In practice, we observe a small group
drawn from

7
00:00:19.482 --> 00:00:22.187
the wider population we're interested in,

8
00:00:22.267 --> 00:00:25.006
and consider what we can infer about that
population.

9
00:00:25.098 --> 00:00:28.889
Let's take an example that we'll return to
throughout this discussion.

10
00:00:28.969 --> 00:00:30.562
Imagine you're a researcher

11
00:00:30.642 --> 00:00:33.334
at a university, studying a particular
forest.

12
00:00:33.414 --> 00:00:35.245
You know that some trees

13
00:00:35.325 --> 00:00:37.826
have parasites. What proportion is
affected?

14
00:00:37.906 --> 00:00:41.649
In practice, you won't be able to examine
every tree in the forest.

15
00:00:41.729 --> 00:00:43.657
There may be physical constraints,

16
00:00:43.737 --> 00:00:46.667
not enough time to survey the whole
forest,

17
00:00:46.747 --> 00:00:49.806
or a lack of funding or staff.

18
00:00:50.186 --> 00:00:52.268
The population is

19
00:00:52.348 --> 00:00:55.728
the complete set of units our study
concerns.

20
00:00:55.808 --> 00:00:57.414
Here, that's the whole forest.

21
00:00:57.802 --> 00:01:01.419
In practice, we'll select a sample.

22
00:01:01.499 --> 00:01:05.115
Here, we select just 24 trees from the
entire forest.

23
00:01:05.195 --> 00:01:09.406
These are the trees we can study and
describe

24
00:01:09.486 --> 00:01:13.777
using statistics, before drawing
conclusions about the whole population.

25
00:01:14.248 --> 00:01:17.705
Once we've selected this sample,

26
00:01:17.785 --> 00:01:21.148
we'll try to draw conclusions about our
population of interest.

27
00:01:21.228 --> 00:01:22.648
Here, the whole forest.

28
00:01:24.008 --> 00:01:26.177
Statistics

29
00:01:26.257 --> 00:01:28.425
allow us to describe variability.

30
00:01:28.505 --> 00:01:29.737
What does that mean?

31
00:01:29.817 --> 00:01:31.423
Within a single forest,

32
00:01:31.503 --> 00:01:33.672
the individual trees aren't all the same.

33
00:01:33.752 --> 00:01:35.640
They differ in size,

34
00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:38.029
shape and species.

35
00:01:38.109 --> 00:01:41.870
Statistics allow us to quantify that
variability.

36
00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:44.822
Here, we're interested in the height of
each tree.

37
00:01:44.902 --> 00:01:47.244
We'll record those measurements in this
table.

38
00:01:47.624 --> 00:01:51.560
The first thing statistics helps us do is
organise our data.

39
00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:54.057
How can we take the data

40
00:01:54.137 --> 00:01:56.604
and arrange them so that we can study
them?

41
00:01:56.684 --> 00:01:59.248
Next, we can compare

42
00:01:59.328 --> 00:02:02.677
the different units in the sample and
population,

43
00:02:02.757 --> 00:02:06.056
and summarise the data using various
measures.

44
00:02:06.136 --> 00:02:08.406
Finally, we may be able

45
00:02:08.486 --> 00:02:10.953
to generalise to the whole population.

46
00:02:11.033 --> 00:02:14.412
We'll explore that when we study
statistical inference.

47
00:02:15.464 --> 00:02:17.358
So, what is a unit?

48
00:02:17.438 --> 00:02:21.145
It's an entity selected for study
according to the study design.

49
00:02:21.225 --> 00:02:23.546
For example, we want to study all

50
00:02:23.626 --> 00:02:26.159
the trees belonging to a particular
population.

51
00:02:26.239 --> 00:02:28.987
Here, that means the whole forest.

52
00:02:29.067 --> 00:02:32.241
A statistical unit could be a single tree.

53
00:02:32.321 --> 00:02:36.428
Each of my observations would then refer
to one tree.

54
00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:38.958
For a different research question,

55
00:02:39.038 --> 00:02:42.312
we might instead study a plot of land.
Why?

56
00:02:42.392 --> 00:02:45.823
Because we might be interested in

57
00:02:45.903 --> 00:02:49.595
soil fertilisation, soil quality,

58
00:02:49.675 --> 00:02:53.316
or the condition of all the trees in a
small area.

59
00:02:53.396 --> 00:02:56.408
The statistical unit depends on the
research question.

60
00:02:56.488 --> 00:02:59.499
It's important to define it carefully

61
00:02:59.579 --> 00:03:02.828
at the outset, because it shapes the rest
of the study.

62
00:03:03.577 --> 00:03:07.712
There are several ways to define a
statistical unit.

63
00:03:07.792 --> 00:03:09.848
Sometimes the choice is less intuitive.

64
00:03:09.928 --> 00:03:12.678
For example, we might consider

65
00:03:12.758 --> 00:03:15.623
a tree observed at different times.

66
00:03:15.703 --> 00:03:18.163
In that case, the same tree

67
00:03:18.243 --> 00:03:21.166
would contribute several observational
units.

68
00:03:21.246 --> 00:03:23.613
The unit in this example

69
00:03:23.705 --> 00:03:28.565
is a tree at a specific point in time.

70
00:03:30.201 --> 00:03:33.112
We also need to define precisely which
population we're studying.

71
00:03:33.192 --> 00:03:35.226
Simply saying "the population" is too
vague.

72
00:03:35.306 --> 00:03:37.047
We need to ask:

73
00:03:37.127 --> 00:03:39.015
where will the study take place?

74
00:03:39.095 --> 00:03:42.768
Here, it's in the forest.

75
00:03:42.848 --> 00:03:44.788
When will we carry out the study?

76
00:03:44.868 --> 00:03:47.187
We need to specify the survey dates.

77
00:03:47.267 --> 00:03:49.625
Again, the date may be a variable,

78
00:03:49.625 --> 00:03:52.125
or it may form part of the observational
unit.

79
00:03:52.217 --> 00:03:55.788
We also need to ask: who will we study?

80
00:03:55.868 --> 00:03:59.332
Here, that could mean a particular tree
species,

81
00:03:59.412 --> 00:04:03.406
or all tree species in the forest, if
that's our focus.

82
00:04:03.486 --> 00:04:04.998
The research question

83
00:04:05.078 --> 00:04:09.094
and study design that define the
population, not the other way round.

84
00:04:09.594 --> 00:04:11.271
I've already mentioned

85
00:04:11.351 --> 00:04:14.018
the idea of a sample.

86
00:04:14.098 --> 00:04:17.942
Here, it's the set of trees selected from
the population.

87
00:04:18.586 --> 00:04:21.325
The sample size can vary because,

88
00:04:21.405 --> 00:04:24.094
in practice, the time available

89
00:04:24.174 --> 00:04:27.467
and the resources for the study may
differ.

90
00:04:27.547 --> 00:04:30.689
We might study just 5% of the population,

91
00:04:30.769 --> 00:04:33.961
or perhaps 20%.

92
00:04:34.041 --> 00:04:36.025
Of course, that depends on

93
00:04:36.105 --> 00:04:38.622
how much effort we can devote to the
study.

94
00:04:38.874 --> 00:04:40.788
Now we need to ask:

95
00:04:40.868 --> 00:04:42.976
can we actually access all the trees?

96
00:04:43.056 --> 00:04:45.748
This brings us to the question of bias.

97
00:04:45.828 --> 00:04:49.152
Imagine we want to survey this forest

98
00:04:49.232 --> 00:04:51.496
and measure the height of its trees.

99
00:04:51.976 --> 00:04:52.896
Trees vary in height.

100
00:04:52.976 --> 00:04:56.352
The population we can actually study

101
00:04:56.432 --> 00:05:00.285
may therefore differ from the one we
originally intended to study.

102
00:05:00.365 --> 00:05:03.205
Here, for example, only a subset

103
00:05:03.285 --> 00:05:06.244
is accessible: the shorter trees.

104
00:05:06.324 --> 00:05:10.078
Perhaps, when collecting our data,

105
00:05:10.170 --> 00:05:13.799
we left some trees unmeasured because they
were too tall.

106
00:05:13.879 --> 00:05:16.159
There's a gap between the population

107
00:05:16.239 --> 00:05:18.856
we want to study and the one we can
actually access.

108
00:05:18.936 --> 00:05:20.782
We need to be careful

109
00:05:20.862 --> 00:05:23.126
that this doesn't introduce bias into the
study.

110
00:05:24.206 --> 00:05:27.726
Here, the accessible population consists
of trees

111
00:05:27.806 --> 00:05:32.212
short enough for us to measure their full
height. Be aware

112
00:05:32.292 --> 00:05:36.386
of the difference between the accessible
population and the target population.

113
00:05:36.466 --> 00:05:38.003
Let's take another example.

114
00:05:38.083 --> 00:05:40.924
Suppose you're conducting a university
travel survey.

115
00:05:41.004 --> 00:05:43.533
You want to know which mode of transport

116
00:05:43.613 --> 00:05:46.482
each student uses to get to university.

117
00:05:46.574 --> 00:05:50.805
Initially, the target is all students. But
the accessible group

118
00:05:50.885 --> 00:05:55.647
consists of those who were sent the
survey.

119
00:05:55.727 --> 00:05:59.604
Some students may be out of reach:

120
00:05:59.684 --> 00:06:03.678
perhaps we didn't send the survey to the
right campus,

121
00:06:03.758 --> 00:06:07.694
or they weren't available when we sent it.

122
00:06:07.774 --> 00:06:10.772
There's a gap between the students

123
00:06:10.852 --> 00:06:14.660
we can reach and those we originally
wanted to study.

124
00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:17.052
Our sample is drawn from that accessible
group.

125
00:06:17.132 --> 00:06:20.022
It consists of the responses we actually
received,

126
00:06:20.102 --> 00:06:23.062
because even among the students

127
00:06:23.142 --> 00:06:25.174
we reached, not everyone was able to
respond.

128
00:06:25.254 --> 00:06:27.586
Those respondents form our final sample.

129
00:06:27.666 --> 00:06:29.781
So there's an initial coverage gap

130
00:06:29.861 --> 00:06:33.208
between the target population and the
population we can reach.

131
00:06:33.288 --> 00:06:37.465
A second gap arises through participation:

132
00:06:37.545 --> 00:06:42.022
not all the students we reached were able
to respond.

133
00:06:42.496 --> 00:06:45.011
There are various ways to tackle these
biases.

134
00:06:45.091 --> 00:06:47.508
The most obvious approach

135
00:06:47.588 --> 00:06:50.005
might be to increase the sample size.

136
00:06:50.085 --> 00:06:52.649
Here, we have a sample on the left and one
on the right.

137
00:06:52.729 --> 00:06:54.705
The sample on the left

138
00:06:54.785 --> 00:06:57.300
covers the population more broadly.

139
00:06:57.380 --> 00:06:59.405
The one on the right, however,

140
00:06:59.485 --> 00:07:01.853
is clustered in one corner.

141
00:07:01.933 --> 00:07:04.203
That's a problem with the study design.

142
00:07:04.283 --> 00:07:07.172
It creates selection bias that we'd like
to address.

143
00:07:07.264 --> 00:07:10.541
Increasing the sample size

144
00:07:10.621 --> 00:07:14.622
may not be enough to remove this bias:

145
00:07:14.702 --> 00:07:18.393
the right-hand part of the population is
still not covered.

146
00:07:18.473 --> 00:07:20.821
So, increasing the sample size

147
00:07:20.901 --> 00:07:23.816
can help, but it isn't always enough.

148
00:07:23.896 --> 00:07:25.469
We need to pay close attention

149
00:07:25.549 --> 00:07:28.132
to the procedures we use to collect our
data.

150
00:07:28.822 --> 00:07:31.994
Once we've selected our samples, we need
to decide

151
00:07:32.074 --> 00:07:35.507
what to study about each statistical unit.

152
00:07:35.587 --> 00:07:38.654
Take a plot of land, for example.

153
00:07:38.734 --> 00:07:42.010
That's my unit. What do we want to know
about it?

154
00:07:42.090 --> 00:07:45.052
We could record the type of grassland,

155
00:07:45.132 --> 00:07:48.566
its ecological condition, whether invasive
species are present, and so on.

156
00:07:48.646 --> 00:07:51.345
Each of these characteristics is a
variable:

157
00:07:51.425 --> 00:07:54.177
something we study for each statistical
unit.

158
00:07:54.257 --> 00:07:56.250
Keep this idea of a variable in mind.

159
00:07:56.342 --> 00:08:00.239
There are different types of variables,
generally grouped

160
00:08:00.319 --> 00:08:04.420
into two families: categorical and
numerical variables.

161
00:08:04.894 --> 00:08:06.932
The simplest type of categorical variable

162
00:08:07.012 --> 00:08:09.680
is a nominal variable.

163
00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:13.974
It assigns observations to descriptive
categories.

164
00:08:14.054 --> 00:08:17.122
For example, grassland might be dry,

165
00:08:17.202 --> 00:08:20.500
wet or flower-rich. But these labels

166
00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:23.763
don't tell us that one type is better than
another.

167
00:08:23.843 --> 00:08:27.106
There is no natural ordering of the
categories.

168
00:08:27.198 --> 00:08:30.000
Other categorical variables

169
00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:33.551
do have an order: for example,
"excellent", "poor", "good",

170
00:08:33.631 --> 00:08:36.793
or "disastrous". These can be placed in a
logical order.

171
00:08:36.873 --> 00:08:40.035
We call this an ordinal categorical
variable.

172
00:08:40.115 --> 00:08:42.145
We can rank the categories

173
00:08:42.225 --> 00:08:44.358
without assigning them numerical values.

174
00:08:44.438 --> 00:08:47.938
There is an order among these
descriptions.

175
00:08:48.030 --> 00:08:51.900
The last category is binary categorical
variables.

176
00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:56.069
These have two possible values: true or
false, 0 or 1,

177
00:08:56.149 --> 00:09:00.184
yes or no. That's what "binary" means.

178
00:09:00.264 --> 00:09:02.783
You may already know the term from
computing.

179
00:09:02.863 --> 00:09:05.200
The other family we'll be studying

180
00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:08.101
is numerical variables.

181
00:09:08.181 --> 00:09:10.821
Quite simply, these are numbers.

182
00:09:10.901 --> 00:09:13.359
For example, a count of beetles

183
00:09:13.439 --> 00:09:16.098
could be 1, 2, 3, and so on.

184
00:09:16.318 --> 00:09:19.802
This kind of numerical variable

185
00:09:19.882 --> 00:09:22.825
doesn't allow arbitrarily many decimal
places.

186
00:09:22.905 --> 00:09:24.259
Here's another example.

187
00:09:24.339 --> 00:09:27.000
At the supermarket, your bill might be

188
00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:30.705
237.18 francs.

189
00:09:30.785 --> 00:09:34.057
But you wouldn't pay 237.18214 francs.

190
00:09:34.137 --> 00:09:38.820
This is a numerical variable with gaps
between its possible values.

191
00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:42.370
You move from one value to the next in
fixed increments.

192
00:09:42.686 --> 00:09:45.775
We call this a discrete numerical
variable.

193
00:09:45.855 --> 00:09:49.462
Other numerical variables, by contrast,
are continuous.

194
00:09:49.542 --> 00:09:53.034
If you take a numerical scale,

195
00:09:53.114 --> 00:09:56.951
you can locate the value you're interested
in,

196
00:09:57.031 --> 00:10:01.445
but there's no fixed gap between possible
values.

197
00:10:01.525 --> 00:10:04.290
You can keep zooming in indefinitely

198
00:10:04.382 --> 00:10:07.182
and still find values in between.

199
00:10:07.262 --> 00:10:10.638
Here, for example, the value appears

200
00:10:10.718 --> 00:10:14.210
to be 18.45 degrees. But if I zoom in
further,

201
00:10:14.290 --> 00:10:17.608
I find more values between 18 and 19.

202
00:10:17.688 --> 00:10:21.353
I can keep zooming in and there will
always be more values.

203
00:10:21.433 --> 00:10:22.700
That's a continuous variable.

204
00:10:22.852 --> 00:10:27.090
The fact that our measurement precision is
limited

205
00:10:27.170 --> 00:10:31.660
doesn't change the continuous nature of
temperature.

206
00:10:32.148 --> 00:10:35.297
Once you've selected the variables of
interest

207
00:10:35.377 --> 00:10:39.705
and collected your data, you need to
organise them.

208
00:10:39.785 --> 00:10:41.909
How might we do that?

209
00:10:41.989 --> 00:10:45.907
Take an observed unit: plot P1. We record

210
00:10:45.987 --> 00:10:50.292
that it's dry, in good condition, and has
no invasive species, and so on.

212
00:10:50.292 --> 00:10:53.471
We have data on many different units,

213
00:10:53.551 --> 00:10:56.984
so we arrange each of those units,

214
00:10:57.064 --> 00:11:00.854
each member of the sample, on a separate
row.

215
00:11:00.934 --> 00:11:03.247
The result is a table,

216
00:11:03.327 --> 00:11:06.099
the simplest way to organise these data.

217
00:11:06.179 --> 00:11:09.336
Each column in the table corresponds

218
00:11:09.428 --> 00:11:12.475
to a variable, while each row is an
observation.

219
00:11:12.555 --> 00:11:15.328
If we look at a particular cell,

220
00:11:15.408 --> 00:11:17.962
where a row and column intersect,

221
00:11:18.042 --> 00:11:21.748
we can see the soil temperature observed
for plot 2.

222
00:11:21.828 --> 00:11:24.601
Its temperature is 16.8 degrees.

223
00:11:24.681 --> 00:11:27.838
This is the simplest way to organise data.

224
00:11:27.918 --> 00:11:30.197
The next step is to describe

225
00:11:30.277 --> 00:11:32.856
the units we've observed.

226
00:11:33.204 --> 00:11:36.584
Let's return to our earlier example,

227
00:11:36.664 --> 00:11:40.654
the travel survey, and describe the people
we observed.

228
00:11:40.734 --> 00:11:45.063
Here, 18 of the 60 respondents are
cyclists.

229
00:11:45.143 --> 00:11:49.812
That means 30% of respondents

230
00:11:49.892 --> 00:11:54.776
mainly travel by bicycle. We also found
that, on average, they travel 4.1 km

231
00:11:54.868 --> 00:11:57.147
to study, work, and so on.

232
00:11:57.227 --> 00:12:00.713
This is descriptive statistics: using
measures

233
00:12:00.793 --> 00:12:04.750
to describe and summarise the data we've
observed.

234
00:12:04.830 --> 00:12:07.529
We'll see that there are several

235
00:12:07.609 --> 00:12:10.440
approaches to statistics that matter for
this course.

236
00:12:10.940 --> 00:12:12.966
After all, our original aim

237
00:12:13.046 --> 00:12:15.627
isn't necessarily just to describe the
data.

238
00:12:15.707 --> 00:12:18.952
We'd also like to generalise to the whole
population.

239
00:12:19.032 --> 00:12:22.056
For example, if 30% of respondents

240
00:12:22.136 --> 00:12:26.102
travel by bicycle, what does that tell us
about the population?

241
00:12:26.182 --> 00:12:30.426
Does 30% of the entire population really
cycle?

242
00:12:30.506 --> 00:12:33.031
Generalising isn't that straightforward.

243
00:12:33.111 --> 00:12:35.328
We always need to

244
00:12:35.420 --> 00:12:39.307
examine the study design and quantify

245
00:12:39.387 --> 00:12:44.224
the uncertainty associated with a sample
that may not represent the population.

246
00:12:44.304 --> 00:12:48.359
We'll cover several ways to do this later
in the course.

247
00:12:48.439 --> 00:12:52.493
To sum up, always start with these
questions.

248
00:12:52.573 --> 00:12:55.790
First, who do we want to draw conclusions
about?

249
00:12:55.870 --> 00:12:58.496
That defines our population.

250
00:12:58.588 --> 00:13:01.707
Next, check whether the target population

251
00:13:01.787 --> 00:13:04.907
matches the population we can actually
access.

252
00:13:04.987 --> 00:13:08.011
They don't always match, so we try to
limit bias,

253
00:13:08.091 --> 00:13:11.353
although some bias will usually remain in
practice.

254
00:13:11.433 --> 00:13:13.407
Then ask who

255
00:13:13.487 --> 00:13:15.937
we actually observed: that's our sample.

256
00:13:16.017 --> 00:13:19.360
We then organise all those data in a
table.

257
00:13:19.452 --> 00:13:22.962
To quantify the uncertainty we've been
discussing,

